thing as {ko'a broda .i ko'e broda}, ie making two predications at once or=
=20
'putting two feet into the same boot' (or some similar expression that=20
somebody once used). The (logical, temporal, etc.) connection between the=
=20
first bridi and the second in {ko'a broda .i ko'e broda} would depend on=20
context, wouldn't it? So, can't the meaning of 'double-FA' expressions be=
=20
considered similarly context dependent?

Out of context, I would assume that both statements are to express=20
prepositions held true (by the speaker). So, such 'double-FA' utterances=20
may have sense or not, depending of course on the predication and the=20
arguments involved.

{catlu fe lo melbi tsani fe lo cmalu rirxe fa ko} -> why not
{se jbena fa mi fa do la cmen.} -> probably dissonant =85 also, sorry for t=
he=20
somewhat ka'u disgusting example :/

Hm. This doesn't make much sense, does it?

